              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.253,
            0.402,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 4.8: Let us explore",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.288,
            0.587,
            0.308
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take some sulfur powder in a glass tumbler."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.311,
            0.492,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Add a small amount of water to it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.333,
            0.379,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.355,
            0.743,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You may have noticed that there is no reaction when sulfur is placed in water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.394,
            0.744,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Substances like sulfur and phosphorus behave differently with air and water than metals. Phosphorus is stored in water as it catches fire when exposed to atmospheric air. These substances are usually soft and dull in appearance. They are neither malleable nor ductile, and they are not sonorous. They are also poor conductors of heat and electricity. These are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "non-metals",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Their oxides are acidic in nature."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.525,
            0.744,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Some other non-metals are oxygen, hydrogen, nitrogen, carbon, etc. These must not be confused with materials such as plastic, glass, wood, rubber, and paper. These materials are not classified as metals or non-metals because they are not elements."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.611,
            0.594,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Metals and non-metals are sub-categories ofMetals and non-metals are sub-categories of substances called elements. An element is a substancesubstances called elements. An element is a substance that cannot be broken down into simpler substances.that cannot be broken down into simpler substances."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.665,
            0.594,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Presently 118 elements are known. ThesePresently 118 elements are known. These elements are the basic building blocks of all matter.elements are the basic building blocks of all matter. Some are naturally occurring, while others areSome are naturally occurring, while others are artificially made in the laboratory, and do not existartificially made in the laboratory, and do not exist in nature. You will study more about elements in higher grades."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
